{"id":"missile_hit","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::missile_hit","source_path":"missile_hit/missile_hit.json","category":"Fire","author":"Autodesk","description":"An exploding missile trail aero simulation with debris","documentation_file":"missile_hit_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Missile Hit\n\n### What it does\n\nAn example aero simulation of a travelling missile which explodes with debris.\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInput for this graph would be the missile geometry. A simple sphere is used for this example. The output is the resulting aero volume comprised of the missile trail, explosion and debris trails.  \n\n### Useful information\n\n- The primary simulation consists of the travelling missile leaving behind a smoke trail, like the one from the smoke trail example. Additionally, fuel is also sourced after 26 frames. The fuel uses an `Ignition Temperature` of 20 degress (equal to `Ambient Temperature`) and thus the explosion occurs as soon as fuel is sourced.\n\n- At the same time that the fuel is sourced and ignited (frame 26), particles are also emitted from the primary geometry. This limited, brief and scattered emission is responsible for the flying debris.  The particles are fed into the aero simulation as a secondary `source_aero`, and a high `Inherit Velocity` is used so they can pull some of the smoke from the explosion volume along with them. Note that the particle aero source also has a custom property \"foo\" associated with it. This is later used to modulate a `turbulence_influence` so that it only affects the particle smoke trails.\n\n- Other notable settings include: Decreased `Buoyancy` to prevent the explosion from rising away too fast, `dissipation_influence` modulated by the inverse of velocity so that dissipation occurs mostly in areas where the smoke coming to rest. `Drag_influence` is also used in modulation with temperature as an extra way to hamper the quick rise of hotter areas.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","file":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","particles":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"aero_volume","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"true"}],"counts":{"nodes":34,"connections":43,"values":153,"compounds":3},"node_types":{"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":4,"Simulation::Influence::modulate_influence":3,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":3,"Simulation::Aero::source_air":2,"Simulation::Influence::dissipation_influence":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":1,"dummy_geo_with_controls":1,"Simulation::Common::file_cache":1,"Simulation::Influence::drag_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::turbulence_influence":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_combustion_settings":1,"Simulation::Aero::source_fuel":1,"Core::Object::set_property":1,"Simulation::Influence::vorticity_influence":1,"Rendering::Arnold::Arnold_standard_volume_mat":1,"particle_simulation":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_sphere":1,"Core::Conversion::scalar_to_vector3":1,"Core::Time::time":1,"Core::Logic::if":1,"Core::Logic::less_or_equal":1,"Core::Graph::terminal":1,"Simulation::Particles::simulate_particles":1,"Simulation::Particles::source_particles":1},"edge_type_pairs":{"Core::Time::time -> Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":3,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve -> Core::Conversion::scalar_to_vector3":3,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property -> Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":2,"Simulation::Aero::source_air -> Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":2," -> Simulation::Particles::source_particles":2,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings -> Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property -> Simulation::Aero::source_air":1,"du